 the same slot is targeted again or if the move creates aliasing, UAF/double-free patterns may emerge. The index is random (not attacker-controlled), limiting direct exploitation.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"input\":\"msg_length\",\"operation\":\"ALLOC calloc(msg_length,1) and read msg_length bytes\",\"risk\":\"LOW\",\"reason\":\"msg_length is checked <= 0x100. calloc zero-initializes. read() uses the same msg_length. No obvious overflow, but the newline-to-NUL replacement at buf[read_ret] could write NUL at buf[msg_length] if read returns full msg_length (off-by-one NUL write at end of buffer).\"}],\"facts\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Binary is PIE, dynamically linked, not stripped, x86-64, compiled with GCC 10.2.0\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F002\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"Security: Full RELRO, NX enabled, Canary, PIE enabled\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F003\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"libc is glibc 2.39 (Ubuntu 2.39-0ubuntu8.8) at /lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libc.so.6\",\"source\":\"RUNTIME\"},{\"id\":\"F004\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg array at 0x4060, 32 slots × 8 bytes = 256 bytes, stores char* pointers to heap-allocated cookie messages\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F005\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size array at 0x4160, 32 slots × 4 bytes = 128 bytes, stores int sizes for each cookie message\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F006\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"cookie_num at 0x41e8, 8-byte long long, initialized to 5 by init(), tracks current cookie count\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F007\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"urandom at 0x41e0, 8-byte FILE*, stores fopen(/dev/urandom) result\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F008\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"init() pre-creates 5 cookies via strdup with sizes 0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40 and sets cookie_num=5\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F009\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie checks cookie_num <= 0x1f and msg_length <= 0x100, allocates calloc(msg_length,1), reads msg_length bytes, stores pointer in msg[cookie_num] and size in msg_size[cookie_num], increments cookie_num\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F010\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"eat_cookie reads 1 random byte from /dev/urandom, computes idx = random_byte % cookie_num (signed idiv), prints msg[idx] via printf(%s), frees msg[idx], then moves msg[cookie_num-1] to msg[idx] and msg_size[cookie_num-1] to msg_size[idx], decrements cookie_num. The old msg[cookie_num-1] slot is NOT zeroed after the move.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F011\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie reads edit_index via scanf(%llu), checks edit_index < cookie_num using signed jl comparison (cmp rdx,rax; jl). There is NO lower bound check (no cmp with 0). The index is used to read msg_size[edit_index] (via [idx*4+msg_size]) and msg[edit_index] (via [idx*8+msg]), then writes msg_size[edit_index]-1 bytes to *msg[edit_index] via read().\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F012\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"read_cookie reads view_index via scanf(%llu), checks view_index < cookie_num using signed jl comparison. NO lower bound check. Dereferences msg[view_index] (via [idx*8+msg]) and prints the string via printf(%s).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F013\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"msg_size is at 0x4160, which is 0x100 (256) bytes after msg at 0x4060. A negative index of -32 on msg[] (idx*8 = -256 = -0x100) would read from msg_size[0]. A negative index of -32 on msg_size[] (idx*4 = -128 = -0x80) would read from msg[16].\",\"source\":\"DERIVED\"},{\"id\":\"F014\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"scanf format for edit_index and view_index is %llu (unsigned long long), but the comparison instruction is 'jl' (signed less-than). A large unsigned value like 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF would be interpreted as -1 in signed comparison, passing the check (since -1 < cookie_num), and then used as a negative array index.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F015\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"GOT is at 0x3f58-0x4000 range, Full RELRO means GOT is read-only after relocation. .bss starts at 0x4020. msg at 0x4060 is 0x40 bytes into .bss.\",\"source\":\"ELF\"},{\"id\":\"F016\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"create_cookie has a newline-to-NUL replacement: after read(), if buf[read_ret] == 0x0a, it sets buf[read_ret] = 0x00. If read returns exactly msg_length bytes (full buffer), this writes NUL at buf[msg_length], one byte past the allocated buffer (off-by-one).\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F017\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie also has a newline-to-NUL replacement at buf[read_ret] using the same pattern, but the read size is msg_size[idx]-1, so the NUL write is at buf[read_ret] where buf is msg[idx] and read_ret <= msg_size[idx]-1.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F018\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"alarm(60) is called in init(), setting a 60-second timeout. The program will receive SIGALRM after 60 seconds.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"},{\"id\":\"F019\",\"type\":\"FACT\",\"description\":\"All cookie message buffers are allocated via strdup (init) or calloc (create_cookie). strdup uses malloc internally. Buffer sizes vary: 0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40 for initial cookies, user-specified (up to 0x100) for created cookies.\",\"source\":\"DISASSEMBLY\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"id\":\"U001\",\"description\":\"Whether the off-by-one NUL write in create_cookie (F016) is exploitable depends on heap layout and glibc 2.39 malloc chunk structure. Needs S2/S3 dynamic analysis.\",\"reason\":\"The NUL write at buf[msg_length] is one byte past the calloc allocation. With glibc 2.39 tcache, this could corrupt the next chunk's metadata, but exploitation depends on specific heap layout.\"},{\"id\":\"U002\",\"description\":\"Whether negative index OOB in edit_cookie/read_cookie (F011/F012) can be used to read/write useful targets depends on what data lies before msg[] in BSS.\",\"reason\":\"Negative indices on msg[] (idx*8) can reach msg_size[] entries and other BSS variables. The exact useful targets (e.g., function pointers, GOT entries) need S2 analysis. GOT is at 0x3f58 which is before .bss (0x4020), but Full RELRO makes GOT read-only.\"},{\"id\":\"U003\",\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie pointer move without zeroing (F010) creates a practical UAF or double-free primitive depends on the random index and the sequence of operations.\",\"reason\":\"The index is random (from /dev/urandom), so the attacker cannot directly control which slot is freed. However, by creating/deleting cookies in specific patterns, the attacker may influence the probability or create exploitable states.\"},{\"id\":\"U004\",\"description\":\"FORTIFY and CET (IBT/SHSTK) status could not be determined from available tools.\",\"reason\":\"checksec did not report FORTIFY or CET status. These may need additional analysis.\"}],\"raw\":{\"elf\":\"ELF64 PIE, x86-64, dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, not stripped, GCC 10.2.0, entry 0x1110\",\"sections\":\"29 sections. Key: .text@0x1110(0x891,AX), .rodata@0x2000(0x266,A), .data@0x4000(0x10,WA), .bss@0x4020(0x1d0,WA), .got@0x3f58(0xa8,WA), .plt@0x1020(0xe0,AX)\",\"symbols\":\"Key symbols: msg@0x4060(256B), msg_size@0x4160(128B), cookie_num@0x41e8(8B), urandom@0x41e0(8B), init@0x1232, create_cookie@0x1399, eat_cookie@0x14e1, edit_cookie@0x1628, read_cookie@0x1751, menu@0x17fd, main@0x187f, _abort@0x11f5\",\"disassembly\":\"Full disassembly of init, create_cookie, eat_cookie, edit_cookie, read_cookie, menu, main collected. Key findings: edit_cookie/read_cookie use signed jl for index check without lower bound; eat_cookie frees then moves pointer without zeroing old slot; create_cookie has off-by-one NUL write potential.\"}},\"vulnerability_report\":{\"schema\":\"VulnerabilityReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"status\":\"COMPLETED\",\"summary\":{\"total_bugs\":3,\"high_severity\":2,\"medium_severity\":1,\"low_severity\":0},\"vulnerabilities\":[{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"title\":\"OOB_SIGNEDNESS_MISMATCH_INDEX_CHECK\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Signedness Error\",\"description\":\"edit_cookie and read_cookie read the user-supplied index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") (unsigned long long), but validate it against cookie_num using a signed 'jl' (jump if less) comparison with no lower-bound check. A large unsigned value such as 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF is interpreted as -1 in signed comparison, passing the check (-1 < cookie_num), and is then used directly as an array index with signed extension (cdqe/movsxd), producing a negative offset into the msg[] and msg_size[] arrays in BSS.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"edit_cookie\",\"read_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (0x4060, 32×8 char* array)\",\"msg_size (0x4160, 32×4 int array)\",\"stdout (0x4020, FILE*)\",\"stdin (0x4030, FILE*)\",\"stderr (0x4040, FILE*)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"edit_index / view_index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\")\",\"entry_function\":\"edit_cookie / read_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides large unsigned value (e.g. 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) as index via scanf(\\\"%llu\\\") at edit_cookie:0x1671 / read_cookie:0x179a\",\"Value stored in stack variable [rbp-0x10] as unsigned long long\",\"Comparison: cmp rdx,rax; jl at 0x1684/0x17ad — signed comparison, no lower bound check, value -1 passes (-1 < 5)\",\"In edit_cookie: idx*4 used to read msg_size[idx] at 0x16b6 (OOB read of int), idx*8 used to read msg[idx] at 0x16d2 (OOB read of pointer)\",\"In edit_cookie: read_size = msg_size[idx]-1 (0x16b9), then read(0, *msg[idx], read_size) at 0x16de — writes attacker-influenced size to attacker-influenced pointer\",\"In read_cookie: idx*8 used to read msg[idx] at 0x17ce (OOB read of pointer), then printf(\\\"%s\\\", *msg[idx]) at 0x17e1 — dereferences and prints OOB pointer as string\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"Index should be validated as unsigned: 0 <= index < cookie_num, using both lower-bound (>= 0) and upper-bound (< cookie_num) checks with unsigned comparison (jb/jae).\",\"actual\":\"Only upper-bound check exists: cmp rdx,rax; jl (signed less-than). No lower-bound check. The %llu format reads an unsigned value but the jl instruction treats it as signed.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing lower-bound check (index >= 0) and incorrect comparison type (signed jl instead of unsigned jb). The signedness mismatch between %llu input format and jl comparison allows negative indices to bypass the bounds check.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"OOB array read via negative index, followed by pointer dereference (read_cookie) or pointer dereference with write (edit_cookie)\",\"address\":\"edit_cookie: 0x16a7-0x16de (msg_size[idx] read, msg[idx] read, read() write); read_cookie: 0x17bf-0x17e1 (msg[idx] read, printf dereference)\",\"expression\":\"edit_cookie: msg_size[idx*4+0x4160] and msg[idx*8+0x4060] with negative idx; read_cookie: msg[idx*8+0x4060] with negative idx\",\"target\":\"BSS data before msg[] array: stdout(0x4020), stdin(0x4030), stderr(0x4040) FILE* pointers accessible at idx=-8, -6, -4 respectively via msg[] OOB\",\"size\":\"edit_cookie: write size = msg_size[idx]-1 (OOB-derived, attacker-influenced); read_cookie: reads until NUL terminator in printf(%s)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Negative index causes out-of-bounds read from msg[] array in BSS. In read_cookie, the OOB-read pointer value (e.g., FILE* at stdout/stdin/stderr addresses) is dereferenced and printed via printf(%s), potentially leaking memory contents at the pointed-to address. In edit_cookie, both msg_size[idx] and msg[idx] are read OOB to determine write destination and size.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In edit_cookie, the OOB-read pointer from msg[negative_idx] is used as the write destination for read(), and the OOB-read int from msg_size[negative_idx] determines the write size. If the OOB pointer value happens to be a valid writable address (e.g., a FILE* struct pointer in BSS pointing to a heap-allocated FILE), data from stdin is written to that address.\"},{\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"In read_cookie, printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[negative_idx]) dereferences an OOB-obtained pointer and prints the memory at that address as a string until a NUL byte. If the pointer value comes from stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* entries, it points to heap-allocated FILE structures, leaking heap memory contents.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the OOB-read pointer value is not a valid mapped address, dereferencing it in either read_cookie (printf) or edit_cookie (read) will cause SIGSEGV.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E001\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1665\",\"description\":\"scanf format string at 0x21c4 is \\\"%llu\\\" (confirmed in .rodata: hex 25 6c 6c 75 at offset 0x21c4), reading unsigned long long into [rbp-0x10]\"},{\"id\":\"E002\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1681-0x1684\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'mov rax,[rip+0x2b67] # cookie_num; cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x1692' — rdx holds the user index, rax holds cookie_num. jl is signed less-than. No preceding comparison with 0 for lower bound. A value of 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (signed -1) passes this check.\"},{\"id\":\"E003\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16a3-0x16b6\",\"description\":\"Index used for OOB access: 'mov rax,[rbp-0x10]; lea rdx,[rax*4]; lea rax,[rip+0x2aaa] # msg_size(0x4160); mov eax,[rdx+rax]' — reads msg_size[idx] with idx from user input, no sanitization after bounds check bypass\"},{\"id\":\"E004\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x16bf-0x16de\",\"description\":\"Pointer dereference and write: 'mov rax,[rbp-0x10]; lea rcx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x298e] # msg(0x4060); mov rax,[rcx+rax]; mov rsi,rax; mov edi,0; call read' — reads OOB pointer from msg[idx] and uses it as write destination in read()\"},{\"id\":\"E005\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x178e-0x179a\",\"description\":\"scanf format string at 0x21c4 is \\\"%llu\\\", same as edit_cookie — reads unsigned long long index\"},{\"id\":\"E006\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17a3-0x17ad\",\"description\":\"Bounds check: 'mov rax,[rip+0x2a3e] # cookie_num; cmp rdx,rax; jl 0x17bb' — same signed jl comparison, no lower bound check\"},{\"id\":\"E007\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x17bb-0x17e1\",\"description\":\"OOB pointer read and dereference: 'mov rax,[rbp-0x10]; lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x2892] # msg(0x4060); mov rax,[rdx+rax]; mov rsi,rax; lea rdi,[rip+0x9cd] # \\\"%s\\\"; call printf' — reads OOB pointer and dereferences it in printf(%s)\"},{\"id\":\"E008\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"edit_cookie/read_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x4060-0x41e8\",\"description\":\"BSS layout analysis: msg at 0x4060, stdout at 0x4020 (offset -0x40 = -64 bytes = idx*8 where idx=-8), stdin at 0x4030 (idx=-6), stderr at 0x4040 (idx=-4). Negative indices on msg[] reach FILE* pointers stored in BSS, which point to heap-allocated FILE structures. These are valid readable/writable addresses, making OOB dereference non-crashing for these specific indices.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm that providing 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as index to edit_cookie/read_cookie passes the jl bounds check at runtime\",\"Verify which BSS data is accessible via specific negative indices and whether FILE* pointers at stdout/stdin/stderr produce valid dereference targets\",\"Determine whether the OOB-read msg_size value for negative indices produces a useful write size in edit_cookie\",\"Confirm whether the off-by-one in create_cookie (BUG-002) can be combined with this OOB to create a more complex corruption chain\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-002\",\"title\":\"OFF_BY_ONE_NULL_WRITE_IN_CREATE_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Size Calculation Error\",\"description\":\"In create_cookie, after read(0, buf, msg_length), the code checks if buf[read_ret] == '\\\\n' and if so writes buf[read_ret] = '\\\\0'. When read() returns exactly msg_length bytes (full buffer, no newline in last position), the index read_ret equals msg_length, which is one byte past the end of the calloc(msg_length, 1) allocation. The NUL write at buf[msg_length] corrupts the first byte beyond the allocated buffer.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"create_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"heap buffer allocated by calloc(msg_length, 1)\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"msg_length and msg_content via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and read(0, buf, msg_length)\",\"entry_function\":\"create_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"Attacker provides msg_length (1-256, checked <= 0x100) via scanf at 0x13ed\",\"calloc(msg_length, 1) allocates exactly msg_length bytes at 0x1416\",\"read(0, buf, msg_length) reads up to msg_length bytes at 0x1443, return value stored in [rbp-0x1c]\",\"If read returns msg_length (full buffer), buf[read_ret] = buf[msg_length] is accessed at 0x1458\",\"Check: if buf[msg_length] == '\\\\n' (0x0a), write buf[msg_length] = 0x00 at 0x146c — this is one byte past the allocation\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"The newline-to-NUL replacement should only access indices within [0, msg_length-1], or the buffer should be allocated with msg_length+1 bytes to accommodate the NUL terminator.\",\"actual\":\"Buffer is allocated as calloc(msg_length, 1) but the NUL write can occur at index msg_length, one byte past the end. There is no check that read_ret < msg_length before accessing buf[read_ret].\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing bounds check on read_ret before accessing buf[read_ret]. The allocation size does not account for the potential NUL terminator at the end of the buffer.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Off-by-one NUL byte write past heap buffer end\",\"address\":\"0x1458-0x146c\",\"expression\":\"buf[read_ret] where read_ret == msg_length, buf = calloc(msg_length, 1)\",\"target\":\"First byte after the calloc allocation — in glibc 2.39, this is the low byte of the next heap chunk's size/metadata field\",\"size\":\"1 byte (NUL)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"MEMORY_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Writing a NUL byte one byte past a heap allocation corrupts the metadata of the adjacent heap chunk. In glibc 2.39 with tcache, the next chunk's size field low byte is overwritten with 0x00, potentially clearing the PREV_INUSE bit or corrupting the chunk size. This can lead to heap metadata corruption when the adjacent chunk is subsequently freed or allocated.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the NUL byte corrupts a critical heap metadata field (e.g., chunk size), subsequent heap operations (malloc/free) may trigger glibc heap consistency checks, causing abort/SIGABRT.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E009\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x13f6-0x13fc\",\"description\":\"Size check: 'cmp rax,0x100; jle 0x140a' — msg_length checked <= 256, then used directly as calloc size and read count\"},{\"id\":\"E010\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1413-0x1416\",\"description\":\"Allocation: 'mov esi,0x1; mov rdi,rax; call calloc' — allocates exactly msg_length bytes (calloc(msg_length, 1))\"},{\"id\":\"E011\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1430-0x1443\",\"description\":\"Read: 'mov rax,[rbp-0x18]; mov rdx,rax; mov rax,[rbp-0x10]; mov rsi,rax; mov edi,0; call read' — read(0, buf, msg_length), return value stored in [rbp-0x1c] (eax)\"},{\"id\":\"E012\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x144b-0x146c\",\"description\":\"Off-by-one: 'mov eax,[rbp-0x1c]; movsxd rdx,eax; mov rax,[rbp-0x10]; add rax,rdx; movzx eax,[rax]; cmp al,0x0a; jne 0x146f; ... mov BYTE PTR [rax],0x0' — accesses buf[read_ret] without checking read_ret < msg_length. If read returns msg_length, buf[msg_length] is one byte past allocation.\"}],\"confidence\":\"HIGH\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm at runtime that read() can return exactly msg_length bytes (e.g., by sending msg_length bytes without a trailing newline)\",\"Verify the heap layout to determine what metadata is corrupted by the NUL write at buf[msg_length]\",\"Determine whether glibc 2.39's tcache chunk layout places critical metadata at the byte immediately following a calloc allocation\"]},{\"bug_id\":\"BUG-003\",\"title\":\"STALE_POINTER_AFTER_FREE_IN_EAT_COOKIE\",\"root_cause\":{\"type\":\"Use-After-Free\",\"description\":\"In eat_cookie, after free(msg[idx]), the code moves msg[cookie_num-1] into msg[idx] and msg_size[cookie_num-1] into msg_size[idx], then decrements cookie_num. The old slot msg[cookie_num-1] is NOT zeroed after the copy — only cookie_num is decremented. When idx == cookie_num-1 (the last cookie is selected), the freed pointer is copied onto itself (msg[idx] = msg[idx]), leaving the freed pointer still in the array at a now-'valid' index (idx < new cookie_num). This creates a stale pointer to freed heap memory that remains reachable through the msg[] array.\"},\"affected_functions\":[\"eat_cookie\"],\"affected_objects\":[\"msg (0x4060, char* pointer array)\",\"msg_size (0x4160, int size array)\",\"freed heap buffer pointed to by msg[idx]\"],\"attack_surface\":{\"input\":\"random_byte from /dev/urandom (not directly attacker-controlled, but cookie_num is attacker-influenced via create/eat operations)\",\"entry_function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"data_flow\":[\"fread reads 1 random byte from /dev/urandom into stack var at 0x152b\",\"idx = random_byte % cookie_num (signed idiv at 0x153e) — index is random but range depends on cookie_num which attacker controls\",\"printf(\\\"%s\\\", msg[idx]) prints cookie content at 0x156e\",\"free(msg[idx]) at 0x158e — frees the heap buffer\",\"msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] at 0x15b0-0x15c6 — copies last pointer to freed slot\",\"msg_size[idx] = msg_size[cookie_num-1] at 0x15e0-0x15fc — copies last size to freed slot\",\"cookie_num -= 1 at 0x1606-0x160a — decrements count but does NOT zero msg[old_cookie_num-1]\",\"When idx == cookie_num-1: msg[idx] = msg[idx] (self-assignment), freed pointer remains in msg[idx], and idx < new cookie_num (idx == old cookie_num - 1 == new cookie_num), so the slot is still 'valid'\"]},\"security_check\":{\"expected\":\"After freeing msg[idx] and moving the last pointer, the old msg[cookie_num-1] slot should be zeroed (set to NULL) to prevent the freed pointer from remaining reachable. Alternatively, when idx == cookie_num-1, the slot should be zeroed before decrementing cookie_num.\",\"actual\":\"Only cookie_num is decremented. The old msg[cookie_num-1] slot retains its pointer value. When idx == cookie_num-1, the self-assignment msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[idx] leaves the freed pointer in place.\",\"missing_or_incorrect\":\"Missing zeroing of msg[cookie_num-1] after the copy operation. No check for the idx == cookie_num-1 edge case where the move is a no-op and the freed pointer persists.\"},\"vulnerable_operation\":{\"operation\":\"Free followed by stale pointer retention in msg[] array\",\"address\":\"0x158e (free) and 0x15c6 (copy without zeroing)\",\"expression\":\"free(msg[idx]); msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]; /* msg[cookie_num-1] NOT zeroed */ cookie_num--;\",\"target\":\"msg[idx] where idx == cookie_num-1 (self-assignment case) — freed heap pointer remains in array\",\"size\":\"8 bytes (pointer)\"},\"impact\":[{\"type\":\"OOB_READ\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"After eat_cookie frees msg[idx] where idx == cookie_num-1, the freed pointer remains in msg[idx] which is now at index == new cookie_num (still < cookie_num after decrement). Subsequent read_cookie(idx) or eat_cookie will dereference the freed pointer via printf(%s), reading from freed/reallocated heap memory.\"},{\"type\":\"OOB_WRITE\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"After eat_cookie frees msg[idx] where idx == cookie_num-1, subsequent edit_cookie(idx) will use the freed pointer as write destination: read(0, *msg[idx], msg_size[idx]-1) writes to freed heap memory. If the memory has been reallocated for another purpose, this corrupts the new allocation.\"},{\"type\":\"CRASH\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"Dereferencing a freed pointer whose heap page has been returned to the OS (or whose chunk has been consolidated) causes SIGSEGV.\"},{\"type\":\"DATA_CORRUPTION\",\"status\":\"CANDIDATE\",\"reason\":\"If the freed buffer is reallocated (e.g., via a subsequent create_cookie call) and then accessed through the stale pointer in msg[], the old and new data structures alias the same memory, causing data corruption.\"}],\"evidence\":[{\"id\":\"E013\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e\",\"description\":\"free(msg[idx]): 'mov eax,[rbp-0xc]; cdqe; lea rdx,[rax*8]; lea rax,[rip+0x2ad9] # msg; mov rax,[rdx+rax]; mov rdi,rax; call free' — frees the heap buffer at the random index\"},{\"id\":\"E014\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1593-0x15c6\",\"description\":\"Pointer move without zeroing: 'mov rax,[rip+0x2c4e] # cookie_num; sub rax,0x1; ... mov rax,[rcx+rax] # msg[cookie_num-1]; ... mov [rcx+rdx],rax # msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1]' — copies last pointer to freed slot, but no instruction zeroes msg[old_cookie_num-1]\"},{\"id\":\"E015\",\"type\":\"STATIC\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x15ff-0x160a\",\"description\":\"Only cookie_num decremented: 'mov rax,[rip+0x2be2] # cookie_num; sub rax,0x1; mov [rip+0x2bd7],rax # cookie_num = cookie_num - 1' — no zeroing of the old last slot\"},{\"id\":\"E016\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x158e-0x160a\",\"description\":\"When idx == cookie_num-1 (random byte % cookie_num == cookie_num-1, probability 1/cookie_num): free(msg[idx]) frees the buffer, then msg[idx] = msg[cookie_num-1] = msg[idx] is a self-assignment (no-op), leaving the freed pointer in msg[idx]. After cookie_num--, idx == new cookie_num, so the slot is still within bounds (idx < new cookie_num is false, but idx == new cookie_num means the slot is at the boundary). Actually idx = old_cookie_num - 1, new cookie_num = old_cookie_num - 1, so idx == new cookie_num. The check idx < cookie_num would fail for this slot. However, if another cookie is created (cookie_num++), this slot becomes valid again with the stale freed pointer.\"},{\"id\":\"E017\",\"type\":\"DERIVED\",\"function\":\"eat_cookie\",\"address\":\"0x1530-0x1541\",\"description\":\"Index computation: 'mov eax,[rbp-0xc]; cdqe; mov rcx,[rip+0x2cac] # cookie_num; cqo; idiv rcx; mov rax,rdx' — idx = random_byte % cookie_num. The probability of idx == cookie_num-1 is 1/cookie_num. With cookie_num=1, idx is always 0 == cookie_num-1, guaranteeing the self-assignment case. The attacker can reduce cookie_num to 1 by repeatedly calling eat_cookie.\"}],\"confidence\":\"MEDIUM\",\"verification_required\":[\"Confirm at runtime that when cookie_num=1, eat_cookie frees msg[0] and the self-assignment leaves the freed pointer reachable after a subsequent create_cookie increments cookie_num\",\"Verify whether the stale pointer can be accessed via edit_cookie or read_cookie after the freed slot becomes 'valid' again\",\"Determine whether glibc 2.39 tcache behavior allows the freed memory to be reallocated and then accessed through the stale pointer\",\"Assess whether the random index truly prevents attacker control or whether cookie_num manipulation gives sufficient control over which slot is freed\"]}],\"non_vulnerabilities\":[{\"function\":\"main\",\"description\":\"Menu choice is read via scanf(\\\"%lld\\\") and validated with a switch statement (cases 1-5, default loops back). No memory operations performed in main itself.\",\"reason\":\"The menu choice is properly bounded by the switch-case structure. Invalid choices simply loop back to the menu. No array access or memory operation uses the menu choice directly.\"},{\"function\":\"init\",\"description\":\"Initializes 5 cookies via strdup with hardcoded strings and sizes. Sets cookie_num=5. Opens /dev/urandom.\",\"reason\":\"All operations in init use hardcoded constants. No user input is processed. The strdup sizes (0x50, 0x30, 0x18, 0x38, 0x40) match the string lengths. No bounds or size issues.\"},{\"function\":\"create_cookie\",\"description\":\"The msg_length check (<= 0x100) and cookie_num check (<= 0x1f) are properly implemented with signed comparison (jle) which is correct for the intended positive value domain.\",\"reason\":\"The upper-bound checks in create_cookie are correct for their purpose. The only issue is the off-by-one NUL write (BUG-002), which is a separate root cause from the bounds checking logic itself.\"}],\"unresolved\":[{\"description\":\"Whether the off-by-one NUL write in create_cookie (BUG-002) can corrupt glibc 2.39 tcache metadata in a way that leads to detectable memory corruption\",\"reason\":\"The exact heap layout depends on the sequence of prior allocations (strdup in init, calloc in create_cookie). The byte at buf[msg_length] could be the prev_size or size field of the next chunk, but this depends on allocation alignment and chunk granularity.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic analysis: allocate cookies of specific sizes, trigger the off-by-one, and inspect heap metadata via gdb to determine what is corrupted\"},{\"description\":\"Whether the eat_cookie stale pointer (BUG-003) is practically reachable given the random index\",\"reason\":\"The index is from /dev/urandom and not directly attacker-controlled. However, the attacker can manipulate cookie_num to influence the probability. When cookie_num=1, the self-assignment case is guaranteed, but the freed slot is at index 0 which equals the new cookie_num (0), making it inaccessible until a new cookie is created.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic analysis: reduce cookie_num to 1 via eat_cookie, then call eat_cookie again to free the last cookie, then call create_cookie to increment cookie_num and verify the stale pointer is accessible via edit_cookie/read_cookie\"},{\"description\":\"Whether negative indices in edit_cookie/read_cookie (BUG-001) can reach useful targets beyond stdout/stdin/stderr FILE* pointers\",\"reason\":\"The BSS layout shows FILE* pointers at idx=-4 (stderr), -6 (stdin), -8 (stdout) via msg[] OOB. These point to heap-allocated FILE structures. Whether dereferencing them produces useful data or crashes depends on the FILE structure contents and heap layout at runtime.\",\"required_next_step\":\"S3 dynamic analysis: provide 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as index to read_cookie and edit_cookie, observe via gdb what pointer value is read from the OOB slot and what happens when it is dereferenced\"}]},\"primitive_reports\":[{\"schema\":\"PrimitiveReport\",\"version\":\"1.0\",\"stage\":\"verification\",\"target_bug_id\":\"BUG-001\",\"status\":\"in_progress\",\"summary\":{\"vulnerability\":\"Signed/unsigned index mismatch in edit_cookie/read_cookie allows out-of-bounds read/write via negative indices\",\"root_cause\":\"Index parameter compared as signed against unsigned boundary, accepting negative indices\",\"bss_layout\":{\"stdout\":\"0x4020\",\"stdin\":\"0x4030\",\"__dso_handle\":\"0x555555558008\"},\"failed_approach\":\"Two-step arbitrary write via __dso_handle fails because post-write code at 0x16e6 re-dereferences corrupted pointer causing SIGSEGV before second edit_cookie call\"},\"primitives\":[{\"id\":\"PRIM-001\",\"type\":\"INFO_LEAK\",\"verified\":true,\"theoretical\":false,\"evidence\":\"Negative index out-of-bounds read via read_cookie successfully leaks BSS segment memory data\",\"details\":{\"function\":\"read_cookie\",\"mechanism\":\"negative_index_oob_read\"}},{\"id\":\"PRIM-002\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE\",\"verified\":true,\"theoretical\":false,\"evidence\":\"idx=-11 locates target to __dso_handle (0x555555558008). GDB confirmed first edit_cookie call with read_size=21844 writes to __dso_handle self-referencing address\",\"details\":{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"index\":-11,\"target\":\"__dso_handle\",\"read_size\":21844}}],\"non_primitives\":[{\"id\":\"PRIM-003\",\"type\":\"ARB_WRITE_TWO_STEP\",\"verified\":false,\"theoretical\":false,\"evidence\":\"Two-step write attempt failed. After first read() completes, code at 0x16e6 re-reads msg[-11] (corrupted __dso_handle pointer 0x43434343434343...) and dereferences it, causing SIGSEGV before reaching second edit_cookie call\",\"details\":{\"function\":\"edit_cookie\",\"failure_reason\":\"post_write_dereference_crash\",\"crash_address\":\"0x16e6\"}}],\"unresolved\":[{\"id\":\"PRIM-004\",\"description\":\"Direct overwrite of stdout (_IO_2_1_stdout_) structure via single negative index write to corrupt vtable or write_ptr for file structure attack\",\"verified\":false,\"theoretical\":true,\"evidence\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"PRIM-005\",\"description\":\"Find alternative index that does not trigger crash-inducing check code path after write\",\"verified\":false,\"theoretical\":true,\"evidence\":\"\"},{\"id\":\"PRIM-006\",\"description\":\"Analyze 0x16e6 instruction logic to craft special 8-byte payload (e.g. preserve valid low address bits) that passes post-write check enabling second write step\",\"verified\":false,\"theoretical\":true,\"evidence\":\"\"}]}],\"target_bug\":\"BUG-001\",\"bug_reports\":[],\"impact_reports\":[],\"strategy_reports\":[],\"final_exploit_path\":\"\",\"final_report\":{}}",
